# Crawlora {{display_name}} Java Client

The official Java client for Crawlora's hosted {{display_name}} API. It calls Crawlora's hosted API with your `x-api-key`; it does not connect to the upstream site directly.

## Install

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>net.crawlora</groupId>
  <artifactId>crawlora-{{platform}}</artifactId>
  <version>{{version}}</version>
</dependency>
```

## Use

Create an account at [crawlora.net](https://crawlora.net/signup?utm_source=maven-central&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=platform-clients&utm_content={{platform}}-java-signup), then open the [Crawlora console](https://crawlora.net/app?utm_source=maven-central&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=platform-clients&utm_content={{platform}}-java-console) to get an API key.

```java
import net.crawlora.{{platform}}.Client;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Map;

try (Client client = new Client(System.getenv("CRAWLORA_API_KEY"))) {
    Object result = client.{{usage_method}}({{usage_params}});
    System.out.println(result);
}
```

Each operation has a direct method that accepts the parameters documented for that endpoint. Use `client.request(operationId, params)` for generic dispatch. `Client.OPERATION_IDS`, `Client.OPERATION_COUNT`, `client.getOperationIds()`, and `client.getOperationCount()` describe this platform's operations only.

For custom hosted API routing and timeouts, use `new Client(apiKey, baseUrl, Duration.ofSeconds(20))`. Text responses are returned as `String`; JSON responses are parsed into Java maps, lists, and scalar values.

